# Harvesting of Infrared Part of Sunlight to Enhance Polaron Transport and Solar Water Splitting

## abstract
There is increasing evidence that defects such as oxygen vacancies are a double‐edged sword for photoelectrochemical (PEC) water splitting. Although surface oxygen vacancies can largely improve the catalytic activity, their bulk counterparts may bind polarons, drag down the carrier transport, and thus degrade the PEC performance. However, it is very challenging to precisely control the spatial and energy distributions of defects. Instead, the infrared part of sunlight, normally discarded in PEC water splitting, is harvested to thermally activate the polarons. With the prototypical BiVO4 photoanode (absorbs blue–ultraviolet light), even when is undoped, a high solar‐to‐hydrogen efficiency of 5.3% is achieved in conjunction with a photothermal substrate (absorbs infrared light) and in tandem with a perovskite solar cell (absorbs red–green). Detailed characterizations reveal that the temperature increase of the system can not only accelerate the polaron hopping, but also activate the polarons bound by defects. This study, by demonstrating the use of the PEC‐inactive infrared part of sunlight to enhance transport kinetics at the device level, allows for panchromatic sunlight harvesting to improve the overall PEC performance.

There is increasing evidence that defects such as oxygen vacancies are a double-edged sword for photoelectrochemical (PEC) water splitting. Although surface oxygen vacancies can largely improve the catalytic activity, their bulk counterparts may bind polarons, drag down the carrier transport, and thus degrade the PEC performance. However, it is very challenging to precisely control the spatial and energy distributions of defects. Instead, the infrared part of sunlight, normally discarded in PEC water splitting, is harvested to thermally activate the polarons. With the prototypical $B i v o_{4}$ photoanode (absorbs blue–ultraviolet light), even when is undoped, a high solar-to-hydrogen efficiency of $5.3\%$ is achieved in conjunction with a photothermal substrate (absorbs infrared light) and in tandem with a perovskite solar cell (absorbs red–green). Detailed characterizations reveal that the temperature increase of the system can not only accelerate the polaron hopping, but also activate the polarons bound by defects. This study, by demonstrating the use of the PEC-inactiv infrared part of sunlight to enhance transport kinetics at the device level, allows for panchromatic sunlight harvesting to improve the overall PEC performance.
## 1. Introduction
Photoelectrochemical (PEC) cells provide an attractive system of harvesting solar energy to split water into hydrogen for a sustainable hydrogen economy in the future.[1,2] Bismuth vanadate $\mathrm{(BiVO_{4})}$ has been regarded as one of the most promising candidates for PEC water splitting with a theoretical photocurrent density $(J_{\mathrm{max}})$ of $7.5\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ under AM $1.5\mathrm{~G~}$ illumination.[3] However, the limited charge carrier transport, sluggish interfacial reaction kinetics, and poor light absorption due to the indirect bandgap severely limit the PEC performance of ${\tt B i V O}_{4}$ .[4,5] In practice, the light absorption and charge transport in such materials are inextricably coupled: a thicker material film is generally associated with a higher ${\mathfrak{h}}_{\mathrm{abs}}$ and lower $\mathfrak{h}_{\mathrm{sep}}$ , and vice versa.[6] The carrier transport in ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ can be described by a small polaron hopping (SPH) mechanism, in which the hopping charge carriers distort the surrounding lattice and thus experience a transport resistance, i.e., a reduced mobility (an upper limit of $0.1{-}1\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{2}\thinspace\mathrm{V}^{-1}\thinspace\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ .[7,8] Recent studies, including ours, further indicated that the positively charged defects in ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ , such as oxygen vacancies (OVs), can bind the negatively charged electron polaron, thus further slowing the majority carrier transport.[9,10] An immediate idea coming to mind would be to eliminate, in reality to reduce, the OVs in the material, as we recently reported. Even with this effort, the photocurrent is still limited by the slow transport of polarons, and thus cannot be enhanced by simply increasing the absorber thickness.[11,12] We recently demonstrated that thermal activation can be the key to overcoming the above limitations, as shown in Figure 1a. Moderate heating of ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ could largely increase the photocurrent of a thicker ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ film to reach and even exceed the photocurrent of a thinner film by a large margin.
Although the positive impact of the operation temperature on the PEC performance has been reported, it has been attributed to various factors including the improved surface reaction rate,[13] hole diffusion,[14] and doping efficiency.[15] Thus, further studies are required for clarification. In addition, these studies on thermally assisted PECs generally relied on energy input in addition to sunlight, for example, from an electric oven or infrared (IR) lamp heating,[16] which will inevitably reduce the practical solar-to-hydrogen (STH) efficiency. Notably, in a typical PEC-photovoltaic (PV) device designed to provide free energy equivalents ${\tt>}1.23\ \mathrm{eV}$ to split water into $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , sunlight beyond $850~\mathrm{nm}$ , accounting for over $30\%$ of the total solar energy, is wasted because of the wide-band-gap semiconductors commonly used for PEC cells and PVs. Thus, capturing this unused IR portion of sunlight by judiciously integrating photothermal materials into such PEC-PV tandem cells as a whole device has become a tempting strategy as well as a large challenge.
In this study, we propose an IR-assisted PEC water splitting strategy to largely broaden the solar spectrum for energy harvesting and increase the STH efficiency. Our aim is to integrally design a novel photoelectrode by combining a photothermal substrate (absorbs IR light) and PEC photoanode (absorbs blue light), and then using it in tandem with a perovskite solar cell (PV, red–green region), as illustrated in Figure 1b. By harvesting solar IR light with a Ni nanoparticle (NP)-based photothermal substrate to increase the temperature of the $\mathrm{BiVO}_{4}@\mathrm{CoPi}$ film and interfaced electrolyte, the charge separation efficiency (determined by the charge transport) was largely enhanced, particularly for thicker films with higher light absorption efficiencies but slower charge transport. As a result, we achieved a photocurrent of $5.19\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ at a bias of $1.23{\mathrm{~V~}}$ (vs Pt wire) and an applied bias photon-to-current efficiency (ABPE) of $1.86\%$ for the photothermal photoelectrodes based on undoped ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ .
An L-shaped design is then proposed to combine such a photothermal PEC electrode with a perovskite PV (FTO/ $\mathrm{NiO}_{x}/$ $\mathrm{MAPbI_{3}/P M M A/P C B M/P P D I N6/A g)}$ . In such a device, the PEC and PV parts are illuminated by two vertically aligned beams separated from simulated sunlight by a customized beam splitter so that we can mine the IR part, albeit energetically insufficient to split water, to achieve photothermally augmented self-powered PEC water splitting with a superior performance $(4.62\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and STH efficiency of $5.7\%$ at $328.15\mathrm{K}$ by self-heating). Furthermore, in-depth studies revealed the dominant role of a thermally activated SPH mechanism in freeing bound polarons from oxygen vacancies. This study broadens our understanding on the thermally enhanced PEC performance and increases the possibility of combining STH and solar-to-thermal conversion technologies for the panchromatic utilization of the solar spectrum.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Integration of Photothermal Conversion into PV/PEC Devices
Figure 2a shows a schematic of the as-prepared $\mathrm{Au/Ni/ITO/}$ $\mathrm{BiVO}_{4}@\mathrm{CoPi}$ photothermal photoelectrode, which can be divided into a photothermal substrate $(\mathrm{Au/Ni/ITO})$ and photoelectrocatalyst $(\mathrm{BiVO}_{4}@\mathrm{CoPi})$ . In this study, we used a sandwich architecture into a photothermal substrate consisting of an IR reflector (Au film), IR absorption layer (Ni NP film), and protective coating of ITO (Sn-doped $\ln_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ ).[17,18] From top to bottom, the structure includes the ITO film, Ni NP film, and Au film. Corresponding sectional scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images and elemental analysis of the photothermal electrode are shown in Figure S1 and Table S1 in the Supporting Information. As illustrated in Figure  2a, the bottom compact Au film was deposited on the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ substrate by thermal evaporation, which functions as a mirror to reflect the incident light over a wide wavelength range into the Ni film to enhance the IR absorption (Figure  2b, black line). The black Ni NP film deposited on the Au film by two-step electrodeposition has the most important role in converting IR light into thermal energy through plasmon resonance absorption.[19,20] As shown in Figure  2b, the as-prepared Au/Ni film (red line) exhibits a strong and wide-range $(400-1800\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) absorption. To protect the Ni NP film from chemical corrosion and scratching, a conductive indium-doped $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ (ITO) film was deposited on top of the Ni NP film by radio-frequency magnetron sputtering.[21] This ITO conductive layer can act as a current collector to efficiently collect the photo-generated electrons from the $\mathrm{BiVO}_{4}@$ CoPi layer. In addition, the final Au/Ni/ITO film exhibited a stronger absorption in the range of $900{-}1750\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure  2b, blue line), which further promoted absorption in the IR region.
Figure S2 in the Supporting Information shows IR thermograph images of the ITO glass and Au/Ni/ITO under direct IR lamp illumination. With only $8~\mathrm{min}$ of IR heating, the latter sample reached a considerably higher temperature of $62.0~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ than that of the ITO control sample, demonstrating its pronounced photothermal effect. We also monitored the local temperature change near the electrode surface using an indirect open-circuit potential method. As shown in Figure S3 in the Supporting Information , there was a temperature difference of at least $5.6\mathrm{~K~}$ between the Au/Ni/ITO surface and electrolyte bulk (for a detailed discussion, see Supporting Information). A temperature difference of $3.8\mathrm{~K~}$ remained after coating the ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ layer. These temperature differences created a temperature gradient and were established by a balance between the heat deposition from the IR photothermal effect and heat diffusion. The increased electrode temperature facilitated the charge transport.
To directly measure the actual solar-to-thermal conversion efficiency under the PEC operating conditions, the composite substrate $(\mathrm{Au/Ni/ITO})$ and control FTO substrate were placed in an electrolytic cell sealed in a polyurethane foam containing $2~\mathrm{mL}$ of a $0.1\textbf{M}$ phosphate buffer solution as a heat reservoir under horizontal simulated sunlight (AM 1.5G, $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ ). The change in electrolyte temperature as a function of the irradiation time is shown in Figure  2c. The as-prepared photo­thermal substrate exhibits an obvious solar-to-thermal conversion as the temperature of the electrolyte increases from 293.15 to $326.55\mathrm{~K~}$ after ${\approx}45~\mathrm{min}$ of illumination.
The solar–thermal conversion efficiency $\eta_{\mathrm{st}}$ of the Au-Ni-ITO photothermal substrate can be estimated from the thermal profile in Figure 2c using the following energy balance equation [22] (see more details in Supporting Information)
$$
\mathrm{m_{i}c_{p,i}}{\frac{d T}{d t}}\mathrm{=Q_{in}}\mathrm{~-Q_{surr,out}}
$$
where $m_{\mathrm{i}}$ and $C_{\mathrm{p,i}}$ are the mass and heat capacity of the electrolyte, respectively, and $Q_{\mathrm{in}}$ and $Q_{\mathrm{surr,out}}$ are the heat input per unit time from the absorber material and heat dissipation per unit time to the surroundings, respectively. When the system temperature is $98\%$ of the final stabilized temperature $(326.55~\mathrm{K},$ after ${\approx}45\ \mathrm{min}$ of irradiation), $\eta_{\mathrm{st}}$ of the Au-Ni-ITO photothermal substrate was estimated to be $\approx70\%$ (see more details in Supporting Information). The photothermal conversion efficiency is excellent, considering that the remaining heat loss can be further reduced by enclosing the device in vacuum.
Unlike IR light, photons in the ultraviolet (UV)–visible region with the above bandgap energy can be efficiently absorbed by an appropriate semiconductor photoelectrode to drive the redox reactions of water, carbon dioxide, etc., affording high-value-added products. Thus, a layer of ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ was loaded onto the photothermal substrate to absorb light below ${\approx}510~\mathrm{nm}$ (UV-blue). Our previous study showed that the poor electron transport may limit the PEC performance of ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ when the thickness of ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ NPs exceeds $450~\mathrm{nm}$ for light illumination from the electrolyte– ${\mathrm{.BiVO}}_{4}$ interface side (denoted as front illumination).[12] Because the thermal effect can help alleviate this limitation, a thick ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ film with a better light absorption can exhibit a better performance under front illumination without the aid of a 3D conductive substrate, as shown in Figure S4 in the Supporting Information. Therefore, we chose a thickness of the ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ NP film deposited on the photothermal substrate of ${\approx}1\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ , as shown in Figure S1b in the Supporting Information. To optimize the surface reaction kinetics for water oxidation, we deposited a thin layer of CoPi cocatalyst on the ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ surface by light-assisted electrodeposition (Figures S1c and S5, Supporting Information).[23] Figure  2d shows LSV curves of the $\mathrm{Au/Ni/ITO/BiVO_{4}@C o P i}$ composite photoelectrode for different illumination times. Under continuous illumination, the photocurrent of the as-prepared $\mathrm{Au/Ni/ITO/BiVO_{4}@C o P i}$ photoelectrode was largely improved with the increase in illumination time. After 45 min of illumination, the photocurrent was improved by $60\%$ from 3.28 to $5.19\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ under an applied voltage of $1.23\mathrm{~V~}$ in a two-electrode system, equivalent to ABPE up to $1.86\%$ (Figure S6a, Supporting Information). In contrast, the $\mathrm{BiVO}_{4}@\mathrm{CoPi}$ photoelectrode deposited on the FTO substrate under the same synthesis conditions did not exhibit obvious change after a long period of illumination, as shown in Figure S6b in the Supporting Information. The photocurrent increment of $1.91\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ is attributed to the extra utilization of solar light beyond $510\ \mathrm{nm}$ by the integration of the photothermal substrate, which demonstrates the advantage of combining photothermal conversion and PEC.
The largely increased ABPE highlights the potential of applying the $\mathrm{Au/Ni/ITO/BiVO_{4}@C o P i}$ photoelectrode in PEC-PV tandem devices. To overcome the adverse heating effect of the photothermal photoelectrode on the photovoltage and stability of the perovskite solar cell, we used an L-shaped tandem device design, wherein the PEC cell and PV cell were perpendicular to each other and irradiated by two light beams separated by an optical splitter from simulated sunlight (Figure 3a). The spectra of the simulated sunlight (AM 1.5G) and two divided beams (calculated based on the splitter reflectance and transmittance in Figure S7 in the Supporting Information) are shown in Figure  3b along with a photograph showing the beam separation. The transmitted light spot, which contains the majority of the region of $300{-}505\ \mathrm{~nm}$ (UV-blue) plus $850{-}2000~\mathrm{~nm}$ (IR) irradiation, is directed to the photothermal PEC cell to heat it from 293.15 to $328.15\mathrm{~K~}$ and power the water splitting reaction, while the reflected beam mainly composed of $505{-}850~\mathrm{nm}$ (green-red) light is steered to the perovskite solar cell $\left(\mathrm{FTO}/\right.$ $\mathrm{NiO_{\itx}/M A P b I_{3}/P M M A/P C B M/P P D I N6/A g)}$ . Figure  3c shows the $I{-}V$ curves of the standalone PV and PEC cells after the optical splitter. The crossing points A and B represent the ideal working points of the integrated tandem cells before and after the PEC cell is photothermally activated by the Ni substrate. Remarkably, after photothermal activation, we obtained a simulated self-powered current density of $4.62\ensuremath{\mathrm{\mA}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cm}}^{-2}$ corresponding to a theoretical STH efficiency of $5.7\%$ (point B), which is considerably higher than that without photothermal activation (point A, $2.14~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}$ , $\mathrm{STH}=2.6\%)$ . When the PV and photothermal PEC components were serially connected (Figure  3d), the tandem device exhibited a strong self-heating effect (up to 327.15 K). The increasing photocurrent was eventually stabilized at $4.28~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $\mathrm{|STH}=5.3\%)$ with the increase in the illumination time. Notably, this STH efficiency, even without using complex 3D conductive substrates, is still among the highest of the category of the reported devices (see Table S2 in the Supporting Information), which demonstrates the prospect of photothermal activation in the PEC field. More details on the construction of the photothermal PEC and PV cells are presented in Experimental Section.
The charge injection efficiency ${\mathfrak{n}}_{\mathrm{inj}}$ and separation efficiency (or transport efficiency) $\mathfrak{h}_{\mathrm{sep}}$ of the $\mathrm{Au/Ni/ITO/BiVO_{4}@C o P i}$ photoelectrode were calculated and are shown in Figure  3e. Considering that water splitting to produce $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is a barrier-crossing and endergonic reaction,[24] the increased temperature enhances the surface reaction rate and shifts the surface reaction equilibrium forward, lending both kinetic and thermodynamic advantages. As expected, the injection efficiency of the $\mathrm{Au/Ni/ITO/BiVO_{4}@C o P i}$ photoelectrode was largely improved after $45~\mathrm{min}$ of illumination (Figure  3e, blue curves). Furthermore, the charge separation efficiency significantly increased from $64\%$ to $94\%$ at an applied voltage of $1\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ (Figure  3e, red curves). The contributions of the improved charge injection and separation efficiencies to the overall increment in the photocurrent are shown in Figure 3f. The increase in photocurrent is mainly attributed to the improved charge separation efficiency, particularly in the low bias region. To evaluate the impact of the increased temperature on the interfacial reaction kinetics without interference from the altered charge separation, we also measured the electrocatalytic performance of $\mathrm{FTO@{\mathsf{CoPi}}}$ . As shown in Figure S8 in the Supporting Information, the measured temperature sensitivity (defined as the photocurrent increase rate per Kelvin) of $\mathrm{FTO@{\mathsf{CoPi}}}$ is $0.98\%/\mathrm{K},$ which is considerably lower than that of $\mathrm{FTO@BiVO_{4}@C o P i}$ $(2.5\%/\mathrm{K})$ . Therefore, the heating-induced performance improvement of $\mathrm{Au/Ni/ITO/BiVO_{4}@C o P i}$ is mainly due to the increased charge separation efficiency. Such an improved charge separation, or rather, charge transport, reduced the probability of self-reduction/oxidation, thereby promoting electrode stability to some extent during the operation (Figure S9, Supporting Information). Notably, numerous studies analyzed the reduction in the interfacial reaction barrier or accelerated kinetics of interfacial absorption–desorption with the increase in operating temperature,[25] but few focused on charge collection in the same context, at least for this system. Thus, in the rest of this paper, we discuss the origin of the heating-induced charge transport improvement in semiconductors.
## 2.2. OV as Polaron Trapping Defect
Polaron hopping has been considered as the primary electron transport mechanism for ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ photoanodes.[8,10,26] The conduction band of this material is mainly attributed to the $\textsf{V}3\mathrm{d}$ characteristic. The formation of an OV leads to localization of electrons on the neighboring vanadium sites to produce $\mathrm{V}^{4+}$ donor states, denoted as $\mathrm{V_{ov}}^{4+}$ .[15] Coincidently, Cooper et  al. reported that the electrons from such OVs can reduce the surrounding V ions to produce negatively charged small polarons, i.e., ${\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{ov}}}^{4+}$ centers, which can further interact with the positively charged OVs to form a bound polaron exhibiting a severely reduced mobility.[10] We believe that such bound polaron formation is one of the origins that imposes a transport limitation on ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ . Although the studied ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ is not intentionally doped, it is challenging to avoid the formation of intrinsic defects such as OVs in practical sample preparation.[9] Therefore, the significant improvement in charge separation efficiency after introducing the photothermal substrate could be attributed to the thermally unleashed bound polaron from the OVs. To verify our conjecture, we systematically studied a series of ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ samples with various OV concentrations, which were prepared by expanding the parameter space of the synthesis conditions.
In general, parameters such as reaction temperature and environment ( $[\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -enriched or -reduced $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas) can largely influence the crystal growth and defect formation. [5,8,27] Therefore, three ${\tt B i V O}_{4}$ samples were prepared by deposition on FTO substrates at temperatures of 430, 460, and $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , denoted as 430-BVO, 460-BVO, and 500-BVO, respectively. 460-BVO was further heated to $460~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and kept for $40\ \mathrm{min}$ in a reductive atmosphere containing $10\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $90\%$ $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ to obtain OVrich 460-BVO-H. The heating conditions were intended to be aggressive to introduce abundant OVs. The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns confirmed the complete conversion of the BiOI phase to the ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ phase (Figure S10, Supporting Information). Three independent characterization techniques were used to qualitatively probe the OV densities in all prepared samples, including electron spin resonance (ESR), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), and photoluminescence (PL) (Figures S11–S13, Supporting Information). All results confirmed that the OV concentration of our samples increased in the following sequence: $460\mathrm{-}\mathrm{BVO}<430\mathrm{-}\mathrm{BVO}<500\mathrm{-}\mathrm{BVO}<460\cdot$ BVO-H. 460-BVO exhibited the lowest amount of OVs owing to the proper annealing temperature. A low reaction temperature of $430^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is insufficient to permit a complete reaction and thus leads to more defects. Because the cooling time is set to $^{\textrm{1h}}$ a high annealing temperature together with a high cooling speed for 500-BVO naturally induce more OVs. More detailed discussions on these aspects are presented in the qualitative analysis of the OV density in Supporting Information.
We evaluate the relationship of the temperature sensitivity of the photocurrent with the OV density. Figure 4a shows the variation in photocurrent with the defect concentration. We used a two-electrode measurement configuration at an applied voltage of $0.8\mathrm{V}$ (vs Pt wire) and $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{3}$ as a hole scavenger to avoid the complication of temperature sensitivity of the reference electrode and surface reaction kinetics (for details, see Supporting Information). At room temperature of ${\approx}293.15\mathrm{~K~}$ , the samples with different OV densities exhibit a wide range of photocurrents from 1.2 to $3.6~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . However, when the temperature is increased to $330.15\mathrm{~K~}_{\mathrm{{z}}}$ the photocurrent distribution is significantly narrowed to the range of $3.46{-}4.5~\mathrm{mA~cm^{-2}}$ . In other words, the thermal activation significantly enhances the photocurrent and reduces the performance difference of the samples, which is in line with the notion of overcoming the transport limitation to reach a saturation photocurrent. At the starting temperature of $293.15\mathrm{~K~}$ , the most obvious anticorrelation of the photocurrent with the OV density was observed. 460-BVO with the lowest O vacancy density exhibits the highest photocurrent of $3.56~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , followed by 430-BVO $(3.1\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ , 500-BVO $\cdot2.24\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}})$ , and 460-BVO-H $(1.2\ \mathrm{mA\cm^{-2}})$ ), coincident with the successively increasing OV density in these four samples. With the heating to $335.15\mathrm{~K~}$ (temperature increase: 45 K), the increasing rate of the photocurrent has a strong positive correlation with the OV density. With the increase in OV density from 460-BVO to 430-BVO, 500-BVO, and 460-BVO-H, the increase rate is $23\%$ , $63\%$ , $68\%$ , and $203\%$ , respectively. For clarity, we define the temperature sensitivity of the photocurrent as the increase rate per Kelvin, which is also indicated in Figure 4a. The values are 0.54, 1.5, 1.62, and $4.5\%/\mathrm{K}$ for the four samples in the above order (order of increasing OV concentration). A similar tendency is observed in the transient $\mathbf{\xi}_{I-t}$ profiles of these samples at increasing operation temperatures from 293.15 to $333.15\mathrm{~K~}$ in Figure S14 in the Supporting Information. These results show the OVs as the underlying factor for the temperature sensitivity of the photocurrent. Thus, as ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{3}$ ensures an almost perfect surface charge transfer, the photocurrent at room temperature is mainly limited by carrier transport, i.e., by the OVs inside ${\tt B i V O}_{4}$ . Thus, with the aid of thermal activation, the transport limitations of OVs are expected to be lifted.
## 2.3. Thermally Activated Electron Transport
To provide further insights into the electron transport limitation in ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4},$ IMPS was performed on all above samples. [12,28] Using this technique, we derive the effective electron diffusion coefficients (for details, see Supporting Information). Two semicircles were observed in the Nyquist plots for all samples. The high-frequency mode, according to our previous study, can be attributed to the trap-free conduction mode, while the other can be assigned as the trap-limited mode. Based on the highfrequency semicircle minima in Figure  4b, the electron diffusion coefficients for the trap-free mode (denoted as $D_{\mathrm{n,~H}}$ can be calculated to be $1.01\times10^{-5}$ , $9.4\times10^{-6}$ , and $8.9\times10^{-6}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ for 460-BVO, 430-BVO, and 500-BVO, respectively. For the low-frequency part (the right part in Figure  4b), the trap-limited mode electron diffusion coefficients $(D_{\mathrm{n,~L}})$ are $8.8\times10^{-8}$ , $4.2\times10^{-8}$ , and $3.1\times10^{-8}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ for 460-BVO, 430-BVO, and 500-BVO, respectively. $D_{\mathrm{n,~H~}}$ remains almost constant in the OV density range of the samples prepared at different temperatures. In contrast, $D_{\mathrm{n,\textsc{l}}}$ largely decreases with the increase in OV concentration. For comparison, the hydrogenated 460-BVO-H with a very high OV concentration exhibits $D_{\mathrm{n,~H~}}$ only slightly decreased by $13.8\%$ compared to 460-BVO (from $1.01\times10^{-5}–8.7\times10^{-6}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , while $D_{\mathrm{n,\bot}}$ sharply decreased by over $75\%$ (from $8.8\times10^{-8}$ to $2.1\times10^{-8}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ . These observations can be explained as the OVs, besides as electron donors, can act as trap centers to capture polarons before reaching the current collector, wherein the positively charged OVs can attract electron polarons to form more strongly bound polarons, resulting in an even lower mobility. A trapping–detrapping process accompanied by a reversible transition between Vov5+ and ${\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{ov}}}^{4+}$ occurs, as illustrated in Figure $4\mathrm{c}$ . This trapping– detrapping process is believed to be detrimental to the charge transport. [10,15] Therefore, an inverse relation between $D_{\mathrm{n,L}}$ and OV density is obtained, as observed.
We confirmed that the OVs are responsible for the traplimited charge transport. Further, we evaluate whether heating can unleash the bound electrons from such defects. To gain a deeper insight into the electron-transport behaviors of the ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ samples with different OV densities, we carried out operatingtemperature-dependent IMPS measurements and plotted the obtained $D_{\mathrm{n,H}}$ and $D_{\mathrm{n,L}}$ versus temperature in Figure 5a. $D_{\mathrm{n,L}}$ of all samples exhibit a considerably higher temperature sensitivity than that of $D_{\mathrm{n,H}},$ which indicates the acceleration of trap-limited electron transport by thermal activation associated with $D_{\mathrm{n,L}}$ The activation energy of $D_{\mathrm{n,L}}$ can be extracted from the temperaturedependent data according to the phenomenological expression[29]
$$
\mathrm{D(T)=D_{0}\ e x p{(-E_{\mathrm{act}}/k_{B}T)}}
$$
where $D_{0}$ is the preexponential factor independent on the temperature, $E_{\mathrm{{act}}}$ is the activation energy of electron diffusion, and $k_{\mathrm{B}}$ is the Boltzmann constant. The $D_{\mathrm{n,~L~}}$ data are replotted in Figure 5b as the logarithm of $D_{\mathrm{n}}$ versus $1/T$ . The calculated $E_{\mathrm{act}}$ of each sample and average values are shown in Figure 5b and Table S4 (Supporting Information). The obtained $E_{\mathrm{act}}$ of $D_{\mathrm{n,~L~}}$ (denoted as $E_{\mathrm{act,~L}})$ with an average value of $0.33\ \mathrm{eV}$ is higher than the measured $E_{\mathrm{act}}$ for $D_{\mathrm{n,H}}$ ( $0.168\ \mathrm{eV}$ in Figure S15 in the Supporting Information) and reported polaron hopping activation energy $(\approx0.28\mathrm{-}0.30\ \mathrm{eV})$ in ${\tt B i V O}_{4}$ .[8,30] This further supports our inference that the existence of OVs resulted in a considerably higher hopping activation energy due to the formation of OV bound polarons. In addition, upon hydrogenation of 460-BVO, $E_{\mathrm{act}},_{\mathrm{~L~}}$ increased from 0.28 to $0.41\ \mathrm{eV}$ in 460-BVO-H. As a result, the PEC performance of 460-BVO-H was largely deteriorated (decreased from 3.6 to $1.2\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ at $298.15\mathrm{~K~}$ , Figure  4a). The deteriorated performance also coincides with the reduced $D_{\mathrm{n,~L~}}$ . In summary, the bound polaron formation with the aid of OVs strongly retards the overall electron transport and reduces the photocurrent response. The temperaturedependent IMPS results also demonstrate that thermal activation is a powerful solution to alleviate the transport limitation due to the trap-limited transport associated with the reversible formation and detrapping of the bound polarons ${\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{ov}}}^{4+}$ .
To further understand the thermal activation of the bound polarons, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was performed under white light illumination to monitor the amount of ${\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{ov}}}^{4+}$ formed via electron trapping by OVs.[15] A simple equivalent circuit (EC) for impedance modelling was used to fit the impedance spectroscopic data (Figure S16, Supporting Information). The fitted results of 460-BVO at different illumination intensities are shown in Figure  5c. There is a capacitance peak at ${\approx}0.8{\mathrm{~V~}}$ in Figure  5c. The capacitance peak is centered at $0.8\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE)), a potential positive with respect to the electrode onset potential $_{(\approx0.35\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs RHE), and agrees with the redox peaks appearing in the cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurement $_{(\approx0.8\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs RHE, Figure S17, Supporting Information ). The capacitance peak difference between the light and dark cases observed in both EIS and CV can be assigned to the light-enhanced redox process of $\mathrm{V_{ov}}^{4+}/\mathrm{V_{ov}}^{5+}$ via electron trapping.[6,15,31,32] As shown in Figure  5c, the amplitude of the $\Delta C$ peak is enhanced with the increase in light intensity, reflecting the aggravated trapping process of the excess photo-generated electrons by OVs as ${\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{ov}}}^{4+}$ . In contrast, as shown in Figure  5d, after increasing the temperature, the capacitance peak difference between the dark and illumination cases almost disappeared, indicating the suppression of electron trapping. This further confirms that even a mild increase in temperature can largely restrain the formation of ${\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{ov}}}^{4+}$ upon illumination and thus prevent photo-generated electron trapping on defects, which coincides with the rapidly increasing $D_{\mathrm{n,L}}$ from the IMPS measurement with the increase in temperature.
## 2.4. Other Factors Related to Thermal Activation
As mentioned above, the thermally enhanced PEC reactivity of ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ was previously attributed to improved hole diffusion and doping efficiencies.[14,15] However, this does not appear to capture the entire mechanism. The argument of thermally improved hole diffusion efficiency from bulk to electrolyte was based on the belief that increasing the temperature could increase the depletion layer width and thus enhance the charge transport.[33] If this is valid, a larger particle size would imply a higher temperature sensitivity of the photocurrent. However, our results showed no apparent correlation between the particle size and temperature sensitivity. Based on the SEM images and Scherrer equation analysis of the XRD patterns, the average particle sizes were estimated to be 50, 94, 98, and $174\ \mathrm{~nm}$ for 430-BVO, 460-BVO, 460-BVO-H, and 500-BVO, respectively (Figure S10 and Table S3 in the Supporting Information, see more details in Supporting Information). 500-BVO with the largest grain size exhibits a similar photothermal activation sensitivity $(1.62\%\mathrm{K})$ to the smallest 430-BVO value $(1.5\%\mathrm{K})$ . Notably, OV-rich 460-BVO-H $(4.5\%/\mathrm{K})$ and 460-BVO $(0.54\%/\mathrm{K})$ , with similar grain sizes, exhibited distinctly different photothermal activations with the increasing temperature (Figure 4a). These results do not support the temperature-induced broadening of the depletion layer as the origin of the heating-enhanced PEC performance of the nanostructured ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ because of its sharply reduced particle size and hole diffusion length. In the conventional view, the increased temperature could indeed increase the doping efficiency owing to the increased ionization rate of defects such as OVs, as shown in Figure 6a.[15] This is in line with our polaronic interpretation in Figure  6b, as the bound polarons (OV trap states) can be thermally unleashed to become free polarons ((i) process in Figure 6b). However, after the OV ionization process, or more precisely, the bound polaron freeing process, the electrons need to be collected through charge transport (process (ii)). Thus, by considering not only the increased free polaron population but also the accelerated electron polaron hopping, our thermally activated polaron transport interpretation provides a more complete picture. Finally, our observation of the negative correlation between the OV density and PEC performance is to some extent contradictory to some literature reports manifesting the positive role of OVs. We argue that the positive role is subject to the condition that the OV concentration is within the proper range. Moreover, our study demonstrated that the ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ photocurrent is very sensitive to the temperature. Abundant OVs in ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4},$ as nonradiative recombination centers, could generate an additional heat, causing an electrode temperature increase. When the temperature during the measurement was not properly controlled (which could have occurred in some studies), a false conclusion might be drawn about the positive role of excessive OVs in the ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ photocurrent.
## 3. Conclusion
In this study, an IR-assisted thermal-activation strategy is demonstrated to enhance the PEC efficiency by designing a photoelectrode composed of a photothermal substrate (IR) and PEC $\mathrm{(BiVO_{4})}$ film (blue) in tandem with a PV cell (red–green). By absorbing the IR part of the solar spectrum to increase the temperature of the $\mathrm{BiVO}_{4}@\mathrm{CoPi}$ film and electrolyte, the photothermal substrate based on Ni NPs could simultaneously improve the charge transport and transfer efficiencies of the ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ NP PEC photoanode. The detailed analysis of the PEC temperature-dependent measurement showed that below $30\%$ of the increase in the PEC performance was due to the improved ${\mathfrak{h}}_{\operatorname{trans}}$ , while over $70\%$ originated from the improved $\mathfrak{h}_{\mathrm{sep}}$ . Based on the electron diffusion coefficients extracted from IMPS, the electron transport of ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ was closely related to the OV concentration. In addition, the increase in the system temperature could largely improve the trap-limited charge transport involving the $\mathrm{V_{ov}}^{5+}/\mathrm{V_{ov}}^{4+}$ redox. According to the EIS analysis, the mounting ${\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{ov}}}^{4+}$ originating from localized electrons under the enhanced light intensity further confirmed the electron-trapping effect of OVs. Upon the increase in the temperature, the increment in the $\mathrm{V_{ov}}^{5+}/\mathrm{V_{ov}}^{4+}$ capacitance peak was largely attenuated, which indicated the suppressed trapping of the photo-generated electrons, which coincides with the IMPS results. Combining together all the results obtained, we conclude that the improved electron transport and device performance by thermal activation were mainly attributed to the freeing of OV-bound polarons and accelerated polaron hopping. As a demonstration of principle, our composite $\mathrm{Au/Ni/ITO/}$ $\mathrm{BiVO_{4}}@\mathrm{CoPi}$ photoelectrode containing the IR photothermal converter of Ni achieved a photocurrent of $5.21\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at 1.23 V in the two-electrode configuration. A high STH efficiency of $5.3\%$ was obtained in the operation in tandem with the PV cell. This study opens a new avenue for harnessing solar IR light to photothermally improve the overall PEC-PV cell performance and thereby provides a basis to integrate PV-PS (Photothermal Substrate)-PEC hybrid devices for the panchromatic harvesting of solar energy.
## 4. Experimental Section
Preparation of Au/Ni/ITO Photothermal Substrate: First, Au film with a thickness of ${\approx}100$ nm was deposited on FTO glass by thermal evaporation with a growth rate of $2\dot{\mathsf{A}}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ . The prepared Au film were cleaned using ultrasonic washer in deionized water, isopropanol, ethanol for 15 min sequentially, and dried at $80^{\circ}C$ . Then, Ni nanoparticle film was deposited on Au film by a two-step galvanostatic electrodeposition in the electrolyte containing $0.58~\mathsf{m}$ nickel chloride and $0.57~\mathsf{m}$ sodium chloride, according to a previous work.[19] Nickel foam plate as counter electrode and Pt wire as reference electrode were applied. The current density was first held at $\mathsf{l.4m A c m^{-2}}$ for $90\varsigma$ and then increased $2.6~\mathsf{m A}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ for $60~\mathsf{s}$ under room temperature $(25-27\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ . In each electrodeposition, the $\mathsf{A u}/$ Ni film was cleansed by DI water and dried at $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere. ITO films were deposited onto $\mathsf{A u/N i}$ substrate by magnetron sputtering.[21] The target was $\ln_{2}{\mathsf O}_{3}–{\mathsf S}{\mathsf n}{\mathsf O}_{2}$ $(90;70\ \mathrm{wt\%})$ ceramic. Prior to the introduction of sputtering gas, the vacuum of the chamber was evacuated to lower than $6\times10^{-6}$ mbar to remove the residual water vapor and air. The flow rates of Ar $(99.999\%)$ were kept at a constant value of 50 sccm. The deposition pressure was $1.5\times10^{-2}$ mbar and the sputtering time was $0.5{\mathrm{~h~}}$ . During the deposition, the substrate was not heated and its temperature was lower than $50~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . After the deposition, the $\mathsf{A u/N i/1T O}$ were further annealed at $300^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $\rceil\boldsymbol{\mathsf{h}}$ in the vacuum chamber with the pressure lower than $5\times70^{-6}$ mbar.
Preparation of $A u/N i/I T O/B i V O_{4}@C o P i$ Photothermal Photoelectrode: BiOI precursor was first prepared on $\mathsf{A u/N i/1T O}$ photothermal substrate from the previous work.[9] Carbon rod and $\sf A g/A g C l$ were used as counter electrode and reference electrode, respectively. The applied potential was $-0.7\vee$ (vs $\mathsf{A g/A g C l})$ and deposition time was 250s. $B i V O_{4}$ films was prepared by a spray-dry-assisted solid-state reaction method, using the above BiOI as precursors. For a typical preparation, $0.5\mathrm{~g~}\mathsf{V O}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{2}$ was first dissolved in $2m L$ DMSO and then diluted with $70~\mathrm{mL}$ absolute ethanol. During the spray drying process, the nozzle was kept moving back and forth at a certain speed, “scanning” the sample surface to ensure even coating. One cycle of deposition was defined to be a back move and a forward move of the nozzle. 50 cycles were performed for each sample. Afterwards, the $\mathsf{A u}/\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{I T O}/\mathsf{B i O l}$ film coated with dried ${\mathsf{V O}}({\mathsf{a c a c}})_{2}$ was transferred into a muffle furnace and heated to $450~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $2h$ with a ramping rate of $2{\mathrm{~}}^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{~}}{\mathsf{m i n}}^{-1}$ . Cobalt phosphate (Co-Pi) was chosen as co-catalyst for demonstration, for its chemical stability and easy preparation. Co-Pi was deposited in $0.05\ m\mathsf{M}\ \mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}\ \slash0.\mathsf{I}$ m Phosphate buffer mixture under illumination with a anodic current of $\mathsf{l}{-}6\upmu\mathsf{A}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ (the applied voltage was $-0.3{\mathrm{~V~}}$ to $-0.15\mathrm{~V~}$ vs $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}_{,}^{\prime}$ for $60~\mathsf{s}$ to 100 s.[9,23] The counter electrode was Pt mesh and the deposition current profile can be found in the Figure S5 in the Supporting Information.
Preparation of L-shape PEC-PV Device: For the part of PEC photoelectrode, a home-made T-shape electrolytic cell sealed in polyurethane foam, which can reduce heat loss, was designed to separate the working electrode $(\mathsf{A u}/\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{I T O}/\mathsf{B i}\mathsf{V O}_{4}@\mathsf{C o P i})$ and the counter electrode (Pt wire). For the part of PV cell, a p-i-n perovskite solar energy device consisted of $\mathsf{F T O/N i O x/M A P b l_{3}/P M M A/P C B M/}$ $\mathsf{P P D}|\mathsf{N}_{6}/\mathsf{A g}$ was prepared by a prenucleation strategy reported in the previous work and encapsulated by epoxy resin.[34] These two parts were connected with wires in series and their irradiated area was ${\approx}0.75~{\mathsf{c m}}^{2}$ .
Preparation of Other Samples: The deposition methods for pure ${\mathsf{B i V O}}_{4}$ on FTO substrate, namely 430-BVO, 460-BVO and 500-BVO, were basically the same to that for BVO on $\mathsf{A u/N i/1T O}$ substrate except for the sintering temperature which was 430, 460, and $500^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 430-BVO, 460-BVO and 500-BVO, respectively. The cooling time for these samples was set to $\textsf{l h}$ . The FTO/CoPi was prepared by electrodeposition in $0.05\times10^{-3}$ m $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}/0.7$ m Phosphate buffer mixture with an applied voltage of $0.8\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}]$ ) for $200\ s$ in a three-electrode system with Pt mesh as counter electrode.
PEC Measurement: Temperature-dependent linear sweep voltammetry was conducted in a two-electrodes system with a quartz cell as container and a $\mathsf{P t}$ wire as counter and reference electrode; the distance between Pt wire and working electrode was ${\approx}1.75c m$ . Electrochemical impedance spectrum, cyclic voltammetry and Mott-Schottky measurements were conducted in a three-electrodes system with a saturated ${\sf A g/A g C l}$ electrode and a platinum electrode were used as reference and counter electrode, respectively. To avoid applied potential correction for the varied temperature, the reference electrode was placed in a separate container kept at room temperature and connected to the electrolyte in the quartz cell using a salt-bridge. At each set temperature, the sample was allowed to equilibrate for $10\mathrm{\min}$ before measurements were taken. The $0.1~\mathsf{m}$ Phosphate buffer $(\mathsf{p}\mathsf{H}=7)$ was used as electrolyte and $0.7~\mathsf{m}$ Phosphate buffer/0.1 m ${\mathsf{N a}}_{2}{\mathsf{S O}}_{3}$ mixed electrolyte was used as electrolyte for sulfite oxidation to acquire surface injection efficiency and charge separation efficiency. Potentials versus RHE were calculated using the Nernst equation $E_{\mathtt{R H E}}=E_{\mathsf{A g/A g C l}}+0.0591\times\mathsf{p H}+0.7976,$ . The illumination source was an Oriel Solar 3A Class Solar Simulator directed at the quartz cell from a certain distance to ensure that the power density of incident light was $\mathsf{l}00\ m\mathsf{W}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ . EIS experimental data were analyzed and fitted using the Z-view software.
Characterization: Microscopic morphology was observed through field emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM, JSM-7800F). The crystal phase of prepared $B i V O_{4}$ films were confirmed by X-ray diffractometer (XRD, D8 ADVANCE). XPS was conducted on PerkinElmer model PHI 5600 XPS system with Mo $\mathsf{K}\alpha$ radiation. ESR measurement was conducted using Bruker A300 operating at room temperature. PL was measured with a spectrometer (LTF-3000, QSpec, Biaoqi, Guangzhou) equipped with an Oxford cryostat designed for optical measurement. Liquid nitrogen was used to cool down the system down to 77K. Intensity modulated photocurrent spectroscopy (IMPS) was studied by Zahner Samnium C-IMPS system with a white lightemitting diode.
